    "https://ru.edu/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Randall-Computer-Science-Posting-S26.pdf": {
      "title": "Position: Assistant Professor of Computer Science / Adjunct Instructor of Computer",
      "description": " \n \nPosition: Assistant Professor of Computer Science / Adjunct Instructor of Computer \nScience  \nLocation: Moore, OK \nStart Date: January 2026 \nSummary:  \n \nRandall University seeks either a full-time or adjunct Professor of Computer Science with \na Biblical worldview beginning Spring 2026. The successful candidate will teach the \nfollowing courses: \nCS 100 Introduction to Programming (3 cr.) \nCS 110 – Object-Oriented Programming & Data Structures (3 cr.) \nCS 200 – Web & Internet Application Development (3 cr.) \nCS 210 – Database Systems & Data Management (3 cr.) \nCS 220 – Software Engineering & Team Project (3 cr.) \n \nThis individual reports to the Provost and Chief Academic Officer and works in \ncooperation with other university faculty members. This is a ten-month, non-tenure track \nrenewable position. \n \nAs a small institution, the ideal candidate is comfortable teaching across multiple areas of \nscience and developing close mentoring relationships with traditional, non-traditional, \nand online students. Class sizes at Randall are small (average 11:1 student-to-faculty \nratio), contributing to a culture of student-centered learning. While this teaching \nassignment includes courses for science majors, the bulk of courses will meet general \neducation requirements. \n \n \nCore Competencies: \n \n● Proficient in teaching physics at the undergraduate level (lecture and lab). \n● Able to teach in at least one other area: Anatomy/Physiology or Earth Science. \n● Committed to integrating faith and learning in the sciences. \n● Comfortable teaching a wide array of student abilities and learning styles. \n● Knowledgeable in the use of instructional technology and laboratory equipment. \n● Strong communication and mentoring skills with undergraduate students. \n \n \n \n \n \nAdministrative/Service Responsibilities: \n \n● Implementing and developing academic intervention policies. \n● Engage in activities to support the mission and vision of Randall University. \n● Providing institutional service in an area within areas of expertise such as \nacademic assessment, intervention, etc. \n● Participate in departmental and university committee work, including \nsupplemental academic instruction committee. \n● Contribute to campus culture, including spiritual formation of students. \n● Participate in ongoing assessment at the course, program, and institutional levels. \nTeaching/Scholarship Responsibilities: \n● Complete 30 workload hours, or equivalent, per academic year. \n● Engage in ongoing professional development and scholarship. \n● Maintain regular office hours. \nQualifications: \n● Master’s degree in Computer Science or a related field required; Ph.D. preferred. \n● Prior teaching experience at the high school, community college, or university \nlevel strongly preferred. \n● Demonstrated commitment to Christ-centered higher education and mentoring \nstudents in their academic and spiritual development. \n● Exhibited evidence of continued professional growth and development. \n● Successful candidate will affirm and support the Mission, Vision, and Statement \nof Faith of Randall University as found here: https://ru.edu/about-randall/what-\nwe-believe/biblical-foundations/ \nResponsibilities: \n● Support students in need of remediation and academic intervention. \n● Participate in departmental meetings, university service, and assessment activities. \n● Maintain regular office hours to support students academically and spiritually. \n● Contribute to the mission of Randall University through service, scholarship, and \nChristian mentorship. \n \n \n \n \n \n \nLogistical Information: \n● Salary is commensurate with experience. \n● Medical and life insurance package with matching 403(b) package. \nSubmit questions or the following materials to the selection committee to the email \ncareers@ru.edu: \n1. Upload a cover letter, which emphasizes his/her strengths with institutional fit. \n2. Submit a curriculum vitae. \n3. Select and submit one piece of additional evidence (statement on philosophy of \nteaching, reference letter, scholarly work, course evaluations). \n4. List of three references. "
    },
    "https://ru.edu/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Job-Description-Business-Faculty.pdf": {
      "title": "Position Title: Full-Time Faculty, Business Department",
      "description": "Job Description \nFull-Time Faculty – Business Department \nPosition Title: Full-Time Faculty, Business Department \nLocation: On-Campus \nReports To: Department Chair, Program Director, Dean, or designated academic administrator \nClassification: Full-Time Faculty \nFLSA Status: Exempt \nPosition Summary \nThe Full-Time Faculty member in the Business Department is responsible for teaching \nundergraduate and/or graduate business courses, supporting student learning and \ndevelopment, contributing to curriculum growth, and advancing the mission of the university. \nThis position requires a strong commitment to academic excellence, student engagement, \nprofessional integrity, and the integration of a Christian worldview into teaching, advising, \nservice, and professional conduct. \nThe faculty member will serve students in a small, private, Christian higher education \nenvironment and should demonstrate a desire to prepare students for ethical leadership, \nprofessional competence, responsible stewardship, and service in business and organizational \nsettings. \n \nEssential Duties and Responsibilities \nTeaching and Instruction \nThe faculty member will: \n• Teach assigned courses in the Business Department, which may include areas such as \nmanagement, marketing, economics, accounting, finance, business law, \nentrepreneurship, analytics, or related business disciplines. \n• Prepare and deliver high-quality instruction in traditional, online, hybrid, and/or \naccelerated formats as assigned. \n• Develop course syllabi, instructional materials, assignments, assessments, and learning \nactivities that align with course and program outcomes. \n• Use effective teaching strategies that promote student engagement, critical thinking, \nethical decision-making, and practical application of business concepts. \n• Maintain appropriate academic rigor while supporting students through clear \ncommunication, timely feedback, and accessible instruction. \n• Evaluate student performance fairly and consistently according to university policies and \nstated course expectations. \n• Incorporate current business practices, technologies, industry trends, and real-world \nexamples into course content. \nChristian Worldview and Mission Alignment \nThe faculty member must demonstrate a clear commitment to the Christian mission and values \nof the university. This includes: \n• Integrating a Christian worldview into teaching, advising, curriculum development, and \nstudent interaction. \n• Helping students understand business as a field that involves ethical responsibility, \nstewardship, service, leadership, and care for others. \n• Modeling Christ-centered professionalism, integrity, humility, respect, and servant \nleadership. \n• Encouraging students to consider how faith informs decision-making in business, \nleadership, management, finances, marketing, and organizational life. \n• Supporting the spiritual and character development of students in a manner consistent \nwith the university’s mission and statement of faith. \nStudent Advising and Mentoring \nThe faculty member will: \n• Advise students regarding academic progress, degree requirements, career goals, \ninternships, and professional development. \n• Mentor students as they prepare for careers, graduate study, leadership roles, and \nservice opportunities. \n• Assist students in developing professional skills such as communication, ethical \nreasoning, problem-solving, teamwork, and career readiness. \n• Maintain regular office hours and be reasonably available to students outside of class. \nCurriculum and Program Development \nThe faculty member will: \n• Participate in the assessment, development, revision, and improvement of business \ncourses and academic programs. \n• Contribute to program review, accreditation-related work, curriculum mapping, and \nstudent learning outcome assessment. \n• Help ensure that business curriculum remains academically sound, mission-aligned, \nprofessionally relevant, and responsive to student and employer needs. \n• Support the development of new courses, certificates, concentrations, or programs as \nappropriate. \nInstitutional Service \nThe faculty member will: \n• Participate in department meetings, faculty meetings, academic planning, and \ninstitutional initiatives. \n• Serve on university committees as assigned. \n• Assist with student recruitment, retention, advising events, assessment activities, and \nother departmental or institutional functions. \n• Represent the Business Department professionally to students, families, alumni, \nemployers, churches, and community partners. \n• Support a collaborative academic culture within the department and across the \nuniversity. \n• Perform other duties as assigned. \nProfessional Development and Scholarship \nThe faculty member will: \n• Remain current in assigned teaching fields through professional reading, continuing \neducation, industry engagement, scholarly activity, or professional development. \n• Pursue opportunities that strengthen teaching effectiveness, subject matter expertise, \nand Christian integration within the discipline. \n• Participate in professional organizations, conferences, workshops, or community \nengagement as appropriate. \n• Maintain professional standards consistent with the expectations of higher education \nand the university’s mission. \nRequired Qualifications \n• Master’s degree in Business Administration or a closely related business field from an \naccredited institution. \n• Commitment to the Christian faith and ability to integrate a Christian worldview into \nteaching and professional responsibilities. \n• Demonstrated ability to teach business courses effectively at the college level. \n• Strong communication, organizational, and interpersonal skills. \n• Ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, students, and administrators. \n• Commitment to student success, academic excellence, and professional preparation. \n• Ability to use learning management systems, instructional technology, and standard \nbusiness software. \nPreferred Qualifications \n• Doctorate in Business Administration or a closely related field. \n• Prior teaching experience in higher education. \n• Professional experience in business, management, leadership, entrepreneurship, \nmarketing, accounting, finance, or related fields. \n• Experience teaching online or hybrid courses. \n• Experience with curriculum development, assessment, accreditation, or program review. \n• Active involvement in a church or Christian ministry setting. \n• Ability to teach across multiple areas of business. \nKnowledge, Skills, and Abilities \nThe successful candidate should demonstrate: \n• A strong understanding of business principles and their practical application. \n• The ability to connect academic content with real-world business practice. \n• A commitment to biblical ethics, servant leadership, and responsible stewardship. \n• A student-centered approach to teaching, advising, and mentoring. \n• The ability to work effectively in a small university environment where faculty often \nserve in multiple roles. \n• Professional judgment, confidentiality, dependability, and integrity. \n• Willingness to support institutional priorities beyond the classroom. \nPhysical and Work Environment Requirements \n• Ability to teach in a classroom, online, and/or office setting. \n• Ability to use a computer and related instructional technology. \n• Ability to attend meetings, events, and university activities as required. \n• Occasional evening or weekend responsibilities may be required for university events, \nrecruitment, advising, or student activities. \nStatement of Faith and Conduct \nThe faculty member is expected to support and uphold the Christian mission, values, and \npolicies of the university. The individual must demonstrate conduct, character, and professional \nbehavior that align with the institution’s Christian identity and educational purpose. \nEqual Employment Opportunity Statement \nThe university is committed to maintaining a Christ-centered academic community and seeks \nemployees who support its mission and values. Employment decisions are made in accordance \nwith applicable laws and institutional policies. \n "
    }
  },
